  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRadar activity early this morning depicts a broken line of\nshowers and thunderstorms stretching from southern Indiana into\nnorthwest Pennsylvania progressing southeastward into the\nforecast area. This concentration of showers and storms will\ncontinue its pursuit southward through the forecast area today\nand peter out this evening over the southern coalfields.\n\nElsewhere in the region, a cold front was analyzed this morning\nto be draped through the lower Great Lakes region. This boundary\nis expected to serve up additional shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment this afternoon along and south of the boundary.\n \nEarly morning CAMs predict afternoon development to fire off \nsometime between 12 and 2 PM across western Ohio and stretch as \nfar east as the lower Ohio River Valley. Isolated cells \nextending down into our far northwestern zones of Perry County \ncould support strong to potentially severe thunderstorms that \nmay cause damaging wind gusts and hail during the late afternoon\ntimeframe. Further upstream where the best destabilization is \nprogged to occur today, convective activity looks to become \noutflow driven towards sunset and fades off quickly with the \nloss of daytime heating this evening.\n\nRainfall totals for today's activity will range from a few\nhundredths of an inch to a few tenths of an inch, and will be\nstrongly influenced by convective trends.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe end of the work week will return to a drier state as the\nregion becomes placed in the warm sector of an approaching\ndisturbance progged for this weekend. Daytime highs will return\nto the 70s and 80s for Thursday and Friday as a result.\n\nStrengthening low pressure over the upper Midwest for the end of\nthe work week will slide eastward with an attending cold front\ndown at the surface. This boundary is slated to travel through\nthe Ohio Valley on Friday and reach the Central Appalachians by\nthe late afternoon/early evening. Widespread showers and storms\nattached to the front will swing through overnight into the\nstart of the weekend, and could bring beneficial rainfall to a\nareas here that are currently facing drought conditions. This\ndisturbance dashes offshore heading into the end of the weekend,\nwith drier weather and seasonable temperatures settling back\ninto the area.",
